  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1229 PM CDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nApproaching the weekend into next week, our synoptic pattern appears \nfar more active, although we will still end the week rather warm \nin the 90s. The 500mb closed low currently meandering along the \nwestern seaboard, is progged to eventually eject eastward over \nthe north-central Great Plains early next week. As it does so, the\nbase of the trough axis should be draped atop the southern High \nPlains this weekend. Once it departs early next week, model \nagreement drastically devolves regarding synoptic progression. \nCurrent thinking is that another low pressure system to our west \nand ridging to our south will place southwest flow or weak ridging\natop the region. Under either scenario, temperatures would still \nbe warm with some locations staying in the 90s, but are expected \nto gradually decrease closer to seasonal averages in the 80s. Low \nprecipitation chances return almost daily thanks to anomalously \nhigh moisture content (peaking Mon-Wed), but current NBM \nprobabilities may be too \"gung ho\" on forecasting widespread rain.\nIt is far more likely under the anticipated synoptic regime to \nhave convection remain more isolated or scattered any given day.\n\nFocusing on this weekend, several environmental parameters including \nlift and moisture (PWATs well above climatological normals) should \nbe favorable for rain/storms across portions of the Panhandles. \nEven though these appear to be lined up favorably with the trough \narrival, there could be a few wrenches thrown in the gears that \nlimit the spatial extent of better rainfall. There is potential \nfor localized dry slots, poorly timed disturbances, and subtle \nupper level subsidence that could all limit rain chances for much \nof the forecast area both days. With all that in consideration, \nhighest confidence in storms is mostly confined to the southern & \neastern Panhandles Saturday-Saturday night (30-50% POPs). \nNorthwest areas have the lowest likelihood of rain, while central \nareas including Amarillo could truly go either way. Sunday could \npresent another rain opportunity for more of the region, but many \nof the aforementioned limiting factors could be in play once \nagain (20-40% POPs favoring the east). \n\nObviously there is still plenty of time for the small details to \nchange and potentially align favorably for more of the forecast \narea to get rain this weekend into next week. Unfortunately \nthough, there are too many variables that are degrading forecast \nconfidence rather than building it at this time. But if we can \ntake full advantage of the moisture available during this time \nframe, beneficial rainfall is certainly within the realm of \npossibility for portions of the region.\n\nHarrel",
